From: openEuler RISC-V Maintainers <noreply@example.invalid>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] tests: recognize post-ACL device-cgroup denial

License: GPL-2.0-or-later
Upstream-Status: downstream-only
Origin: openEuler RISC-V packaging

The permissions test first proves that user bin receives EACCES before an ACL
entry grants access to the synthetic block-device inode.  Linux then performs
the POSIX ACL/DAC check before its device-cgroup permission hook.  A hardened
container can therefore return EPERM after the ACL succeeds, while an
unrestricted host continues far enough to return ENXIO for the nonexistent
device.

Accept exactly those two post-ACL outcomes.  Keep the pre-ACL EACCES assertion
and every character-device, FIFO, directory, ownership, and CAP_FOWNER check
unchanged.

Remove this patch when upstream carries an equivalent expectation or the
locked build container grants read access to the synthetic block device
major/minor 91:64 and consistently reaches ENXIO.

---
 test/root/permissions.run | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/test/root/permissions.run b/test/root/permissions.run
--- a/test/root/permissions.run
+++ b/test/root/permissions.run
@@ -246 +246 @@ test if the device exists.
-	>~ .*hdt: No such device or address$
+	>~ .*hdt: (?:No such device or address|Operation not permitted)$
